Senator Dick Durbin of Illinois is the  number two Democrat in the Senate and a  member of the Judiciary Committee he  joins us from Capitol Hill senator good  to see you thank you so much for joining  us good to be with you so as of this  morning does it look like Brett  Kavanaugh will become the next Supreme  Court justice well it's not clear of  course we have four members of the  Senate three Republicans in one Democrat  who have not announced what their final  vote will be there's a test vote a  procedural vote this morning which will  give us an indication yes we just went  went through all that for our viewers  let me ask you the fellow Democrat Joe  Manchin of West Virginia will he vote  for Cavanaugh you know set through a  dozen or more meetings with Joe on the  subject he's kept his own counsel  he's obviously weighing this thing from  a personal point of view he understands  the historic impact I could not tell you  as I stand here how Joe's gonna vote are  you trying to lobby senator Manchin well  you know it's a little different in the  Senate twisting arms doesn't work a bit  the best thing you can do is to just try  to talk it through in terms of the issue  itself in this case the nomination the  impact that's going to have on the party  on the nation certainly on the state  that the senator represents but you  don't twist arms at this level Democrats  have made it clear senator they are not  happy with the FBI inquiry what do you  wish they would have done well let me  tell you what a disappointment I said in  that room yesterday and went through  this scant  survey they made of about ten witnesses  maybe nine at most and I thought to  myself even these witnesses were  identifying people who should have been  talked to the FBI didn't they were  clearly under orders to limit this  investigation and as a result there were  many witnesses eight of them suggested  by dr. Ford 20 by Miss Ramirez who were  never even contacted by the FBI it was a  very disappointing effort senator is  there anything that Democrats in  particular could have done differently  to make this a late less painful process  for dr. Ford I don't think so I think we  went out of our way to try to bend to  her wishes you remember when she first  came forward she branded her letter  confidential and made it clear she  didn't want her identity disclosed  senator Feinstein respected that request  did not want to victimize her another  time and so time pass  and that's been used against senator  Feinstein I'd say unfairly if she was  trying her best  specifically senator based on this FBI  inquiry there is still no one that  corroborates dr. Ford's account of what  happened in fact there is even some  testimony by Leland Kaiser her friend  that she she told the FBI she felt  pressured to change what she had said  why wasn't there any corroboration who  should have been interviewed that you  think could have corroborated her  account well there could have been at  least eight people that were suggested  by dr. Ford that could have been  interviewed I certainly think that Marc  judge in this circumstance should have  been brought in under oath and asked  questions there were elements of his  statement that I can't disclose but they  certainly raise questions which should  have been pursued what's your feeling  about his op-ed this morning judge  Cavanaugh as he admits that he got a  little too emotional but he says he can  return to being in his words even keeled  open-minded and independent were you  moved by what he had to say no I'm not I  understand his emotion and his anger  that this has to be a terrible ordeal  for him and his family and I understand  that part but the fire in his eyes when  he turned into this partisan scree is  something I'm not going to forget I  cannot imagine the Supreme Court justice  who would be so partisan make such wild  claims and then ask to be put on a bench  and trusted his temperament when I agree  with Justice 